A cast of the decoded field only covers sizes that already fit in an `int32`. `int64(info.GetSize())` is already what the mapper does, and `2,147,483,647` is the largest value that call can ever see. A 3 GiB file never becomes an `int32`, so there is nothing to convert: Daytona's decoder rejects the JSON first and returns no `FileInfo`.

The other fields can stay on `toolbox.FileInfo`. Only `size` has to be read from the raw JSON as an `int64`. On a 200 response, take that number, then put a size the `int32` decoder accepts back into the body so the rest of the metadata still decodes:

```go
var raw map[string]json.RawMessage
if err := json.Unmarshal(body, &raw); err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
var size int64
if err := json.Unmarshal(raw["size"], &size); err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
raw["size"] = []byte("0")
patched, err := json.Marshal(raw)
if err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
var info toolbox.FileInfo
if err := json.Unmarshal(patched, &info); err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
mapped, err := daytonaFileInfo(&info, "stat")
mapped.Size = size
```

For a 12-byte file, `size` is `12`. For `"size": 3221225472`, `size` is `3221225472` and `toolbox.FileInfo` still supplies the name, path, mode, owner, and timestamp. A 404 never enters this path.

